Z||000000||Brian84SE||02-06-2000||08:34 PM||||Does anyone know a way to hook up a CD to the Factory am/fm/cassette without going through the cassette mechanism with a "dummy" cassette?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||02-06-2000||08:40 PM||||About all I can think of, would be to get a CD changer. It would have its own display and controls, but you can buy models that send signal out on FM frequencies.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||02-06-2000||08:47 PM||||I finally just went with a Firebird CD player. They are backlit orange to match the stock colors. About $250 at the wreckers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Brian84SE||02-06-2000||09:01 PM||||Was this an am/fm/cd?  I like the idea of the backlight color being the same.  What year(s)  firebird should I be looking for?  Would one from any GM work?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||02-06-2000||10:50 PM||||On he record, I htink the Firebird head unit looks GREAT.  A cautionary note...That particular deck has the "Theftlock" feature, meaning that without a code, the unit will not work when removed and reinstalled. (Basically whenever current is interrupted.) Make sure that your used parts source has the code to reactivate the system, and make sure they will give full refund if the code does not work.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||02-06-2000||10:53 PM||||Also,if you are looking for a lower budget method to add CD to your stock system, I saw a portable CDplayer mount in the Damark catalogue that has a built in radio transmitter that plays through FM radio.  Can't vouch for how it works, but seemed like a cool idea.||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||02-07-2000||12:23 AM||||yes, AM/FM/CD. 6 AM, 12 FM stations. Seek, no scan, random, many have speed variable volume (faster-&gt;louder).<P>I think the year range is 92 and up. Any junkyard selling the CD players will disable the theftlock. They do on the one's I've gotten. It wouldn't be worth $250 otherwiise, right?<P>The connectors are different (just ask for them), but it has all of the same connections plus it supports the steering wheel controls &lt;g&gt; and cd changer. It fits in the same location. <P>On the automatics, you can't get a CD into it when it's in park. Small price to pay. The brackets are a bit different but that's a minor issue. <P>If you don't mind the white back-lighting, there are more choices. The Pontiac's have the amber lights. Not sure about the others.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 02-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||ggarsk||02-07-2000||02:23 AM||||I wouldn't try one of those things that transmitt on FM.....they are a bunch of s***. nuff said.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||02-07-2000||05:49 AM||||I have a Kenwood FM modulated CD changer.  It works great.  Never a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||02-07-2000||11:07 AM||||In the next few months, I may try to "hardwire" my CD changer into my Delco head unit. I'll post the results. <BR>For all of the truly out-of-control... I'm also planning to install a pre-out / pre-in loop, so that I can connect an active crossover between the source and the built in amp. The point of this is to strip off all of the bass, and run it to a separate amp. The internal amp will be able to "coast", if all is has to amplify are mids and above. Why? I just wanna see if I can. ||||reg||6
Z||000010||SCCA FIERO||02-07-2000||08:16 PM||||Terryk, how does the stereo know how fast the car is going? Did you have to hook something up to the speedo or something? My friend has a factory CD player in his 97 S-10 and it does that... faster you go, louder the stereo gets. And would this CD player work with factory subwoofer from the Fiero?||||reg||5
Z||000011||Monkeyman||02-07-2000||08:32 PM||||SCCA FIERO-Pulses from the alt or RPMs or something, although I'm probably wrong.  I'm not very technical.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||02-07-2000||08:54 PM||||We've had/got vehicles with the speed sensitive stuff. The radio gets louder and softer. Windshield wipers do the same thing. The faster you go, the more often they wipe(while in delay mode), the slower the less often they wipe. I don't know what controls them. <BR>Although I've never had one, I've heard the cd changers that run on FM, work pretty good. Its the little black box that you plug into your discman and transmists FM that isn't very good.||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||02-07-2000||10:47 PM||||Yep, uses the VSS pulses.||||reg||1
Z||000014||87GTBro||02-08-2000||12:09 AM||||I bought a portable CD player made by Emmerson Technologies from WAL-MART for $60 that transmits on FM frequencies.  It works great!  It even has 10 second Anti-skip which works well for the cost.<P>Worth looking into.<P>I just set it in the seat next to me, pop in a CD and tune to an unused frequency.  Whalla!  It works!!||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||02-08-2000||12:19 AM||||I've never heard of an FM-modulated deal for a Discman.  I have one that is a permanent mount for the car.  I guess you'd say that it's a little more "professional" than just hooking up a Discman, but a lot more expensive, too.  It's a Kenwood, model KDC-C512FM in case anyone is interested.||||reg||1
